{
  "dom_id": "step-q163197",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=3.$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{2}{3},3)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
